(* FAST reproduction of the spec_av residualization bug that blocks fp2.
 *
 * This is a VALID reversible program:  [fp_dyncond_bug]((nil . d)) = 'one
 * (for any non-nil d; 'two for d = nil), with the store cleared at the end.
 *
 * But specializing it with spec_av and running the residual FAILS:
 *   comp = [spec_av]((fp_dyncond_bug . ('S . nil)))    -- succeeds, small comp
 *   [comp](d)                                          -- raises "error in update"
 * although the correct answer is 'one.
 *
 * Root cause: the conditional `if D ...` tests the DYNAMIC input half, so
 * spec_av takes the dynamic-test branch (SPEC-STEP-AV 'cond, L922-) and
 * DYNAMICIZE-ALL's the store, then residualises the conditional.  That
 * residualisation is UNSOUND: it produces a non-reversible residual (a
 * reversible-update `^=` whose runtime slot is neither nil nor equal to the
 * assigned value -> rupdate "error in update").
 *
 * This is the GENERAL form of the 2nd-projection (fp2) `var=Elem` failure:
 * under self-application spec_av's own conditionals (e.g. MKAV's `if MkS`)
 * become dynamic, hit this same path, and produce a broken compiler.  Unlike
 * the real fp2 (~4 min), this reproduces at fp1 scale in well under a second,
 * so it is the iteration harness for fixing dynamic-cond residualisation.
 *
 * spec_av specializes programs of the form (('var.I).(Body.('var.J))) whose
 * input variable holds (Src . d): here Src (the static half) is unused (nil)
 * and the conditional dispatches on d (dynamic).  Contrast ri_min/ri_seq,
 * whose conditionals test the STATIC op, so they avoid this path and fp1 works.
 *
 * See HANDOFF_fp2.md and memory second-futamura-projection-status. *)
read X;
  cons Sv D <= X;
  Sv ^= Sv;
  if D then R ^= 'one else R ^= 'two fi (=? R 'one);
  D ^= D;
write R
